steps.body:
A function created inside another function keeps access to the outer function's variables, even after the outer function has returned. That pairing of a function and the variables it remembers is a **closure**.

```javascript
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return () => {
    count++;
    return count;
  };
}
const counter = makeCounter();
console.log(counter(), counter(), counter()); // 1 2 3
```

`makeCounter` is a **function factory**: it builds and returns a new function. The returned arrow still sees `count`, so each call bumps it. Nothing else in the program can reach `count`, which makes it private state.

Every call to the factory runs its body again, so each returned function gets its own separate `count`. Two counters never share a total.

Factories can also take arguments that the returned function remembers:

```javascript
function makeMultiplier(k) {
  return (x) => x * k;
}
const double = makeMultiplier(2);
console.log(double(21)); // 42
```

A closure keeps the variable itself, not a copy of its value. That explains the `var` loop bug: arrows created in a `for (var i ...)` loop all share the same `i`, so they all see its final value.

example.code:
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return () => {
    count++;
    return count;
  };
}

function makeMultiplier(k) {
  return (x) => x * k;
}

const first = makeCounter();
const second = makeCounter();
console.log(`first: ${first()} ${first()} ${first()}`);
console.log(`second: ${second()}`);

const triple = makeMultiplier(3);
console.log(`triple(7): ${triple(7)}`);
console.log(`triple(10): ${triple(10)}`);

